          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (strncpy) where the source and destination buffers may overlap, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Overlapping memory regions in such operations can lead to undefined behavior, which may cause unpredictable program behavior or security issues. The destination buffer is a pointer, and there is no evidence that the source and destination are guaranteed to be separate. Although the source and destination are both non-null and there is an explicit null-terminator assignment after the copy, the risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap remains. This is a well-known issue with standard C library functions like strncpy, which do not support overlapping buffers.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying strings where the source and destination buffers might overlap, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory areas safely. Replace the string copy operation with a call to `memmove`, ensuring you copy the correct number of bytes and handle null-termination as needed.\n\n```cpp\n// Replace strncpy with memmove for overlapping buffers\nsize_t copy_len = AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1;\nmemmove(market_name, name, copy_len);\nmarket_name[copy_len] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\n___\n"
          },
