            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a memory copy operation. The code uses a standard memory copy function, and dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C++ standard. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the destination buffer's capacity is not known or proven to be safe. Both the source and destination are confirmed to be non-null at this point, so the main risk is the potential for overlapping memory regions, which is not allowed for memcpy. This type of issue can cause unpredictable program behavior, data corruption, or crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `std::memmove` instead of `std::memcpy`, as `std::memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. For example, replace the original memory copy call with:\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if the source and destination buffers overlap, the operation will be performed safely and predictably. For more information, see the [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices/) and [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html).\n\n___\n"
