            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's actual size is not known at compile time, and the code copies up to a maximum length minus one without explicit evidence that the buffer is large enough to hold that many bytes. The use of a C-style string as the source reduces the risk of source/destination overlap, but does not address the core issue of potential buffer overflow. There is no explicit guard or check to ensure the destination buffer is at least as large as the maximum string length specified. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings if the source is longer than the allowed length. These factors make this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow, ensure that the destination buffer is at least as large as the maximum string length specified. If possible, use safer string copy functions that require the actual size of the destination buffer, or add explicit checks before copying. Additionally, ensure the destination string is null-terminated after copying, as `std::strncpy` does not guarantee null-termination if the source is longer than the specified count.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure destination buffer is large enough before copying\nif (market_name != nullptr) {\n    std::strncpy(market_name, empty.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    market_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Explicit null-termination\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, consider using `snprintf` if the destination is a character array:\n\n```cpp\nif (market_name != nullptr) {\n    std::snprintf(market_name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, \"%s\", empty.c_str());\n}\n```\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\n___\n"
