          "message": {
            "text": "Detected Prohibited C Function",
            "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the function `std::ctime()` is considered insecure because it is not thread-safe. This means that if it is called by two or more threads simultaneously, it can cause data races, leading to undefined behavior and potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use thread-safe alternatives to `std::ctime()`. In C++, this could be the `std::put_time()` function, which is part of the `<iomanip>` library. This function formats the time in a safe manner, preventing potential data races.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <chrono>\n#include <iomanip>\n\nint main() {\n    auto now = std::chrono::system_clock::now();\n    auto itt = std::chrono::system_clock::to_time_t(now);\n    std::cout << std::put_time(std::localtime(&itt), \"%c\") << '\\n';\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::chrono::system_clock::now()` is used to get the current time, and `std::put_time()` is used to format it. This code is thread-safe and does not have the vulnerabilities associated with `std::ctime()`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<iostream>`\n- `<chrono>`\n- `<iomanip>`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "rocm_smi/src/rocm_smi_gpu_metrics.cc"
