            "text": "Problematic C function detected (memcpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a memory copy operation where the source and destination may overlap, which can result in undefined behavior according to the C++ standard. The analysis detected that the source and destination expressions may refer to overlapping memory regions due to data flow between them. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the source and destination are not simple string literals or constant data, which would otherwise reduce the risk. Additionally, the source may be null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. The destination is not a pointer-based member, so the risk of a null destination is low. Overall, the combination of possible overlap and lack of protective checks makes this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use `std::memmove` instead of `std::memcpy`. `std::memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory areas safely. Replace the memory copy operation as follows:\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(&header_value, &tmp_header_info, sizeof(metrics_table_header_t));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy will be performed correctly even if the source and destination overlap. For more information, see the [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices/) and [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html).\n\n___\n"
