          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's capacity is not known at this point in the code, and the number of bytes to copy is not proven to be within the buffer's bounds. The code copies data from a string's C-style representation into a character buffer using a length that is not directly tied to the actual size of the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflows if the provided size is larger than the buffer's actual capacity. Although the source is a C-style string (which reduces the risk of overlap), and there is an explicit null-terminator assignment after the copy, the absence of a guard to ensure the count does not exceed the buffer's size means the risk remains. The destination is a pointer, and there is no evidence that the count is derived from the buffer's actual capacity, which further increases the risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow, ensure that the number of bytes copied does not exceed the actual capacity of the destination buffer. If possible, pass the actual size of the buffer as an argument, or use safer string copy functions that require the buffer's size. For example, if you know the buffer's size, use it directly:\n\n```cpp\n// Before:\n// std::strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), sizeOfBuff-1);\n\n// After:\nsize_t buff_capacity = /* actual size of buff */;\nstd::strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), buff_capacity - 1);\nbuff[buff_capacity - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nIf the buffer's size is not known, refactor the code to ensure it is always passed alongside the buffer pointer. Alternatively, consider using `std::string` or safer C++ string handling functions to avoid manual buffer management.\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\n___\n"
          },
