          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as high risk because the destination buffer's actual size is not known at compile time, and the number of bytes to copy (len-1) is not proven to be within the bounds of the destination buffer. The use of a C-style string as the source reduces the risk of source/destination overlap, but does not address the core issue of potential buffer overflow. There is no explicit guard to ensure that the length argument does not exceed the destination buffer's capacity, and there is no evidence that the length is derived from the buffer's actual size. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. These factors indicate a genuine vulnerability that could result in buffer overflow or string truncation issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate this buffer overflow risk, ensure that the number of bytes copied does not exceed the actual size of the destination buffer. If the destination buffer's size is known, use it directly in the copy operation. Additionally, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings. For example, if you know the size of `name` is `name_size`, you can do:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(name, vendor_name.c_str(), name_size - 1);\nname[name_size - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf the size is passed as `len`, ensure that `len` is not greater than the actual buffer size, and always null-terminate:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(name, vendor_name.c_str(), len - 1);\nname[len - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nAlternatively, consider using safer string copy functions such as `snprintf` or C++ string assignment if possible:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(name, len, \"%s\", vendor_name.c_str());\n```\n\nFor more information, see [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow) and [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html).\n\n___\n"
          },
