            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code copies a string into a destination buffer without any explicit check to ensure that the destination pointer is not null. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe from overlap issues, but the analysis indicates that the destination pointer may be null at this point in the code. If the destination pointer is null, calling this function will result in undefined behavior, potentially causing a crash or other unpredictable results. There is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this, and the function does not appear to guarantee that the destination is valid before the copy operation.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the destination pointer (`cpu_info`) and its `model_name` member are not null. This prevents undefined behavior if the pointer is invalid. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_info != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    cpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nIf `cpu_info` can be null, handle the error appropriately (e.g., return an error code or log the issue). Always ensure the destination buffer is valid before performing buffer operations.\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n___\n"
