            "text": "Problematic C function detected (memcpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping source and destination buffers in a memory copy operation. The code copies data from one structure to another using a standard memory copy function, but dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C++ standard. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the source and destination are not simple string literals or constant arrays, which would otherwise reduce the risk. Additionally, the source may be null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. The destination is a pointer type, and its capacity is not explicitly known, which means the safety of the operation cannot be guaranteed by construction.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior caused by overlapping memory regions, use `std::memmove` instead of `std::memcpy`. `std::memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping source and destination buffers safely. Replace the memory copy function as follows:\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(metrics_table, &metrics_tbl, sizeof(amdsmi_hsmp_metrics_table_t));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if `metrics_table` and `&metrics_tbl` overlap, the operation will be performed safely according to the C++ standard.\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices - Input Validation](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices/)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\n___\n"
          },
