            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (strncpy) where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, which prohibits overlapping memory regions for such operations. The analysis detected a feasible data flow from the destination to the source, indicating a real risk of overlap. There is no evidence of a guard or explicit check to prevent this, and the source is not a string literal or constant, which would have reduced the risk. The destination buffer is explicitly NUL-terminated after the copy, which is good practice, but does not mitigate the undefined behavior risk from overlapping buffers. The source may also be null, but the main concern here is the potential for memory overlap, which can cause unpredictable program behavior or security issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk from possible overlapping source and destination buffers, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`. The `memmove` function is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. If you need to ensure NUL-termination, you should manually set the last byte to `\\0` after the copy, as `memmove` does not guarantee this for string data.\n\n**Example fix:**\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->build_date, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\ninfo->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if the source and destination buffers overlap, the copy will be performed safely and the destination will be properly NUL-terminated.\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Secure Coding Practices - Input Validation](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices/)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n- [CWE-587: Assignment of a Fixed Address to a Pointer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/587.html)\n\n___\n"
          },
