          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (strncpy) where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, which prohibits overlapping memory regions for this function. The analysis detected a feasible data flow path where the destination and source could refer to the same or overlapping memory, increasing the risk of memory corruption or unpredictable program behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the source pointer could potentially be null, which further increases the risk. The destination buffer's capacity is not explicitly known, and there is no evidence that the length argument is derived from the destination's actual size. All these factors contribute to the high confidence in this being a real issue that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling the string copy function. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. For example:\n\n```cpp\n// Replace strncpy with memmove if overlap is possible\nmemmove(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n```\n\nAlternatively, add explicit checks to guarantee that the source and destination do not overlap, or refactor the code to avoid such situations. Always ensure that the source pointer is not null before copying.\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\n___\n"
          },
