            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a high-confidence undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a call to strncpy. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions, which can cause undefined behavior according to the C standard. There is no evidence of explicit null-termination after the copy, and no guard is present to prevent underflow or overlap. The source is a general identifier, and the destination's capacity is not explicitly known. The risk is further supported by dataflow analysis indicating a possible overlap. The only mitigating factor is that the source is not a string literal or constant, which would have reduced the likelihood of overlap, but this does not apply here.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior caused by overlapping source and destination buffers in `strncpy`, ensure that the source and destination do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Additionally, always ensure the destination buffer is properly null-terminated after copying.\n\n```cpp\n// Before:\nstrncpy(info->version, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n\n// After: Use memmove if overlap is possible, and ensure null-termination\nmemmove(info->version, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\ninfo->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf you are certain that the source and destination never overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and add assertions if possible. Always ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated to prevent string-related vulnerabilities.\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\n\n___\n"
