          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (strncpy) where the source and destination buffers may overlap, which can result in undefined behavior according to the C standard. The analysis detected that the source and destination may be related through data flow, increasing the risk of overlap. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this, and the destination buffer's capacity is not clearly defined. Additionally, the source pointer may be null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. These factors indicate a real risk that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate this issue, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead, which is safe for overlapping memory regions. For example, replace the use of `strncpy` with `memmove` if you cannot guarantee non-overlapping buffers:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->version, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n```\n\nAlternatively, add explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination do not overlap before performing the copy. If the source can be null, add a null check before copying:\n\n```cpp\nif (vbios.vbios_ver_str != nullptr && info->version != reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str)) {\n    strncpy(info->version, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nFor more information, see [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_overflow) and [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html).\n\n___\n"
          },
