          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (strncpy) where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, which prohibits overlapping memory regions for this function. The analysis detected a feasible data flow from the destination to the source, indicating that overlap is possible. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this, and the source may also be null, which further increases the risk. The destination buffer's capacity is not explicitly known, and there is no evidence that the length argument is derived from the destination's actual size. These factors all contribute to the risk of undefined behavior and potential security issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate this issue, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead, which is safe for overlapping memory regions. For example, replace the use of `strncpy` with `memmove`:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->name, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.name), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n```\n\nAlternatively, add explicit checks to guarantee that the source and destination do not overlap, or refactor the code to avoid such situations. Always ensure that the source pointer is valid and not null before copying.\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n- [CWE-587: Assignment of a Fixed Address to a Pointer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/587.html)\n\n___\n"
