          "message": {
            "text": "Detected Vulnerability in C Function (strlen)",
            "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using functions that take the length of the string as an argument, such as `strnlen()`, which will only read up to a specified number of characters.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `strlen(container_type_name[i])` with `strnlen(container_type_name[i], MAX_LENGTH)`, where `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum expected length of your string.\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming MAX_LENGTH is defined and container_type_name[i] is a string\nsize_t length = strnlen(container_type_name[i], MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library (`<cstring>` in C++, `<string.h>` in C), so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
