            "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary runtime checks, and can lead to undefined behavior if used improperly.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `fprintf` is used, which is generally considered safe. However, if user-controlled input is passed to the format string parameter, it can lead to a format string vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using C standard library functions that do not perform bounds checking or other necessary runtime checks. Instead, use safer alternatives that are provided by the C++ standard library or other libraries that provide the necessary checks.\n\nFor the `fprintf` function, if user-controlled input is passed to the format string parameter, ensure that the input is properly sanitized and validated before use. Alternatively, avoid using user-controlled input in the format string parameter altogether.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `fprintf` function is used safely as no user-controlled input is passed to the format string parameter. However, if user-controlled input is to be included in the error message, use the `%s` format specifier and pass the input as an argument to `fprintf`, like so:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = /* user-controlled input */;\nfprintf(stderr, \"[ERROR] Invalid buffer as read length was exceeded: %s\\n\", user_input);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `stdio.h`: for the `fprintf` function and `stderr` file pointer.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure 