          "message": {
            "text": "Detected Prohibited C Function",
            "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are known to be insecure. These functions are insecure because they do not perform bounds checking, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. The `printf` function is one such function that can lead to this vulnerability if not used properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that perform bounds checking. For the `printf` function, a safer alternative is the `snprintf` function, which allows you to specify the maximum number of characters to be written to the output buffer, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to replace `printf` with `snprintf`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char buffer[50];\n    int a = 10;\n    snprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"Value of a is %d\", a);\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", buffer);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `snprintf` writes the formatted string to `buffer`, but it will not write more than `sizeof(buffer)` characters, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the `stdio.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
          },
