          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy operation where the destination buffer may be a null pointer, and there are no visible checks to ensure that the destination is valid before copying. The source is a C++ string's c_str(), which is safe and non-null, but the risk comes from the destination potentially being null. The absence of a guard or validation for the destination buffer increases the risk of undefined behavior, such as a segmentation fault or crash. The classification specifically highlights the lack of null checks, and the analysis did not find evidence that the destination is always valid.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the string copy operation, ensure that the destination buffer (`info.name`) is not a null pointer. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior:\n\n```cpp\nif (info.name != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(info.name, name.c_str(), std::min((unsigned long)AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, name.length()));\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination:\n    info.name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle error: destination buffer is null\n}\n```\n\nThis prevents dereferencing a null pointer and avoids a potential crash. Always ensure that the destination buffer is properly allocated before copying data into it.\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices/)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n___\n"
          },
