          "message": {
            "text": "Detected Prohibited C Function",
            "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflow. In this case, the `sprintf` function is used, which is known to be insecure because it does not perform bounds checking on the destination buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `sprintf` that perform bounds checking to prevent buffer overflow. In C++, this could be `snprintf`, `std::string` or `std::stringstream`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `snprintf`:\n\n```cpp\nchar command[COMMAND_SIZE];\nsnprintf(command, sizeof(command), \"/proc/%d\", process_info_list[it].pid);\n```\n\nIn this code, `snprintf` will write at most `sizeof(command)` characters to `command`, including the null terminator. This prevents buffer overflow because `snprintf` ensures that the destination buffer is not overrun.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>` for `snprintf`\n- `<vector>` or similar container library for `process_info_list`\n- `<sys/types.h>` for `pid_t` type\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
