            "text": "\"Prohibited C Function Found\"",
            "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of memory corruption. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary safety measures.\n\nIn the provided code, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if user-controlled input is passed directly into it. However, in this specific case, there is no direct vulnerability as the string is hardcoded and does not contain any format specifiers.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions that do not perform bounds checking or other safety measures. Instead, use safer alternatives that are provided by the C++ standard library or other trusted libraries. \n\nFor the `printf` function, if you need to format strings, consider using safer alternatives such as `snprintf`, `fprintf`, or `sprintf` with proper size specification. Always ensure that user-controlled input is properly sanitized and escaped before it is included in a format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this specific case, since the string is hardcoded and does not contain any format specifiers, there is no direct vulnerability. However, to follow secure coding practices, you could replace the `printf` function with `puts` or `fputs`, which do not have format string vulnerabilities:\n\n```cpp\nputs(\"+=======+\"\n     \"+=============+=============+=============+============\"\n     \"==+=========================================+\\n\");\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code requires the C standard library (`<cstdio>` or `<stdio.h>` in C++) to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
