            "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_3)` is identified as a potential vulnerability sink. However, without more context, it's hard to provide a specific mitigation strategy. If this function is using prohibited C functions internally, it could be a source of vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nThe general mitigation advice for this vulnerability is to avoid using prohibited C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library or other trusted libraries. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout knowing the internal implementation of `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_3)`, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if it's using prohibited C functions, those should be replaced with safer alternatives.\n\nFor example, if the function is using `strcpy()`, it could be replaced with `strncpy()` or better yet, use C++ strings.\n\n```cpp\n// Unsafe\nchar dest[20];\nstrcpy(dest, source);\n\n// Safer\nchar dest[20];\nstrncpy(dest, source, sizeof(dest) - 1);\n\n// Even safer (C++ way)\nstd::string dest = source;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to list the library dependencies required by the code example. However, if the `enable_debug_level()` function is part of a library, that library would be a dependency.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
